Actually, wait. Important subtlety: the run that gets cancelled in the ghost scenario — was it queued, or was it a duplicate dispatch cancelled while queued? In the ghost evidence: "the duplicate's check-suite (registered at dispatch time... 12s after the 21:39:48 dispatch) is left in_progress". So the run was cancelled while queued, after its check-suite registered but before jobs materialized. When a queued run is cancelled, GitHub does NOT conclude its check-runs (no jobs to run → check-runs stay pending forever). If cancelled after materialization (jobs exist), GitHub concludes the check-runs as cancelled — a clean terminal state.

So the guard: poll until status != 'queued' before cancelling. If still queued after timeout → defer (do not cancel; the run remains queued, will be re-selected next tick — since it's still active).

